    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on a schema migration and i'm a bit stuck on the normalization part. can you walk me through how to actually get a table from 2NF to 3NF? i get the basic idea of removing redundancy but i keep tripping up on how to identify the dependencies. thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To move from 2NF to 3NF, focus oneliminating transitive dependencies where a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute that is related to the primary key. First, identify partial dependencies by removing redundancy, as you've already done, and then look for chains where one non-key attribute determines a third attribute (e.g., in an Employee table, if \"Department\" implies \"Location,\" but the PK is \"Employee ID\" + \"Department,\" a 3rd anomaly might exist if Location depends on Department). You can formalize this by listing all functional dependencies and ensuring no non-key attributes determine other non-key attributes. Once isolated, extract those dependent attributes into separate tables while retaining the valid foreign key relationships.",
