      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it, that clears up the split. what about when the redundancy isn't as obvious? like if i have a bunch of extra columns that don't seem to belong to the primary key but aren't clearly pointing to another column either. how do i tell if something is just \"clutter\" in the schema versus something that actually belongs in its own table?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To distinguish between schema clutter and valid normalization opportunities, analyze each column to determine if it is \"non-prime\" and if it determines or is determined by other non-prime columns. If a column appears to store derived information that could be stored elsewhere without losing data integrity, it is almost certainly redundant. For example, an email address in a product table might seem irrelevant until you realize duplicate products share the same email, indicating a transitive dependency that belongs in a separate 'users' table. The easiest way to test is to mentally ask: \"If I delete this column, does the business process still function correctly and safely?\" If not, it likely serves a vital logical link; if yes, it was probably accidental noise.",
      "source": "target",
